UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 24, Lesson 1: How laws are made', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Laws are rules for the whole community, made mainly through parliament processes (proposed, debated, voted, approved). Laws differ from family rules in scale and enforcement.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSort: wear school hat (school rule); stop at red light (law); make bed (family rule).\n\nFEEDBACK\nSchool; law; family.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking anyone can invent a national law overnight skips parliament processes.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nDiagram a simplified law-making flow from a published civics source. Give two examples of laws that keep people safe.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Laws are rules for the whole community, made mainly through parliament processes (proposed, debated, voted, approved). Laws differ from family rules in scale and enforcement.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Diagram a simplified law-making flow from a published civics source. Give two examples of laws that keep people safe. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using law on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking anyone can invent a national law overnight skips parliament processes.. Correction: School; law; family.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking anyone can invent a national law overnight skips parliament processes. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating law on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with law, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about law with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for law. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"School; law; family is correct because This matches the taught method for law. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking anyone can invent a national law overnight skips parliament processes","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","School; law; family"],"question":"Sort: wear school hat (school rule); stop at red light (law); make bed (family rule). Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for law; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of bill and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Thinking anyone can invent a national law overnight skips parliament processes","Laws are rules for the whole community, made mainly through parliament processes (proposed, debated, voted, approved)","School; law; family"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with law?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named bill and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name bill and explain why it matters only, without linking it to law","Apply law to new material and name the bill and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Thinking anyone can invent a national law overnight skips parliament processes"],"question":"Which response best applies law with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses sources, places or historical/geographic evidence appropriately for this lesson on law. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking anyone can invent a national law overnight skips parliament processes","School; law; family","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail"],"question":"Which response best uses sources, places or historical/geographic evidence for law?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a specific source or place detail and say what it shows about law","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail","Thinking anyone can invent a national law overnight skips parliament processes"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about law?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Laws are rules for the whole community, made mainly through parliament) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking anyone can invent a national law overnight skips parliament processes","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail","Example simplified flow: idea → bill → debate → vote → law (details vary by level)"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about law?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check facts, acknowledge limits of the evidence, and avoid inventing details","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete"],"question":"Which action keeps sources, places or historical/geographic evidence accurate for law?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example","Thinking anyone can invent a national law overnight skips parliament processes","Name bill and explain why it matters only, with no linked example","Diagram a simplified law-making flow from a published civics source"],"question":"Which independent response best applies law to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about law with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating law on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using law on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for law. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with law, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking anyone can invent a national law overnight skips parliament processes.. Correction: School; law; family.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y4-w24-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise law.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that